.SH NAME
goblin-skiff-client \- client-side helper for Goblin Skiff
.SH SYNOPSIS
MOSH_KEY=KEY
.B goblin-skiff-client
[\-v]
[\-A]
[\-X]
[\-L \fISPEC\fP]
[\-D \fISPEC\fP]
[\-\-fips\-crypto]
[\-\-stream-delay=\fIMS\fP]
[\-\-stream-bandwidth=\fIBPS\fP]
[\-\-state-zstd-dict=\fIFILE\fP]
[\-\-state-sample-log=\fIFILE\fP]
IP PORT
.br
.B goblin-skiff-client
\-c
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \-\-image\-codecs
prints supported image transport codecs and exits. The wrapper uses this
query to negotiate palette/DjVu support with the server.
.PP
\fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP is a helper program for the
.BR goblin-skiff (1)
remote terminal application.

\fBgoblin-skiff\fP itself is a setup script that establishes an SSH
connection, runs the server-side helper \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP,
and collects the server's port number and session key.

\fBgoblin-skiff\fP then executes \fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP with the server's IP
address, port, and session key. \fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP runs for
the lifetime of the connection.

The 22-byte base64 session key given by \fBgoblin-skiff-server\fP is supplied
in the MOSH_KEY environment variable. This represents a 128-bit AES
key that protects the integrity and confidentiality of the session.

For constructing new setup wrappers for remote execution facilities
other than SSH, it may be necessary to invoke \fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP
directly.

With the \-c option, \fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP instead prints the number of
colors
of the terminal given by the TERM environment variable.

The \-v option will print some debugging information on standard
error.  More instances of this flag will result in more debugging
information.  If standard error is not redirected from the terminal,
the display will be corrupted and quickly become unusable.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-connection\-version
Print a machine-readable MOSH VERSION line containing the Goblin compatibility
protocol, release and build, then exit. The wrapper uses this query to identify
the selected client. Both endpoints also exchange identity in authenticated
session traffic; \-v reports the peer identity.
.TP
.B \-\-socks5\-proxy=\fIHOST:PORT\fP
Carry authenticated UDP through a SOCKS5 UDP ASSOCIATE proxy.
IP may instead be a hostname, resolved at the proxy. Use [ADDRESS]:PORT
for an IPv6 proxy. No direct fallback, SOCKS fragmentation or proxy
username/password authentication is supported. The TCP control association
is nonblocking and automatically recreated after disconnection; it does
not carry the UDP data. Session keys and state are retained across recovery.
With \-c, validate the option without connecting. The wrapper also uses
this option to proxy SSH startup; invoking the client alone does not do SSH.

.TP
.B \-\-udp\-relay
Use the comma-separated independent relay keys in GOBLIN_SKIFF_RELAY_KEYS, ordered
nearest-to-farthest (one to four hops). IP and PORT name the first relay;
MOSH_KEY remains the final destination's key. The client consumes and unsets
the key environment variables. This option is normally supplied by the
wrapper's \-J/ProxyJump setup, not invoked manually. It can be combined with
\-c to preflight support. With \-\-fips-crypto every outer layer also uses
the configured FIPS provider; no fallback is allowed.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-kitty, \-\-no\-sixel
Disable the named local graphics protocol, including its startup mascot
probes and output. Disabling Kitty also suppresses rendering of remote Kitty
images locally. These options override forced mascot and preview formats;
a disabled forced format falls back to ASCII.

.TP
.B \-\-mascot=\fIFORMAT\fP
Select auto, kitty, sixel, ascii, or none for the inline startup mascot.
The mascot remains in native scrollback when the first remote frame arrives;
graphics retention depends on the local terminal.

.TP
.B \-\-show\-mascot=\fIFORMAT\fP
Preview the mascot without connecting. Auto uses ASCII without terminal
queries. The graphics-disable options take precedence.


.TP
.B \-c
Print the number of colors supported by the local terminal and exit.

.TP
.B \-v
Print debugging information. More instances increase verbosity.

.TP
.B \-\-fips\-crypto
Use the \fBaes128-gcm-v1\fP wire format and require cryptographic operations
from a configured OpenSSL 3 FIPS provider. This must match the server mode.
The option also works with \fB-c\fP so setup wrappers can verify local provider
availability before starting a remote server.

.TP
.B \-\-tmux\-control
Enable tmux -CC control-stream rendering and raw command input. The setup
wrapper must first negotiate \fBtmux-control-v1\fP with the server. May also
be used with \fB-c\fP to verify local support before remote setup.

.TP
.B \-L \fISPEC\fP
Request a local TCP forward. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1)
for the format.

.TP
.B \-D \fISPEC\fP
Request a local SOCKS5 dynamic forward. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B \-A
Request SSH agent forwarding.

.TP
.B \-X
Request X11 forwarding. The local
.B DISPLAY
environment variable must be set, and the local X authority file must
contain MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 data for that display.

.TP
.B \-\-stream\-delay=\fIMS\fP
Coalesce forwarded stream bytes for up to \fIMS\fP milliseconds before
sending.

.TP
.B \-\-stream\-bandwidth=\fIBPS\fP
Cap forwarded stream payload bytes per second.

.PP
When both peers advertise zstd support, every serialized terminal
state update uses zstd level 22. There is no size threshold or zlib
comparison. Initial packets remain zlib-compressed until support is
negotiated.

.TP
.B \-\-state\-zstd\-dict=\fIFILE\fP
Use \fIFILE\fP as the local zstd dictionary for state updates. Files
produced by
.BR goblin-skiff-compile-dictionary (1)
are stored compressed with zstd level 22 and are decompressed before
use. This only enables dictionary compression if the peer advertises
the same dictionary id.

.TP
.B \-\-state\-sample\-log=\fIFILE\fP
Write received, uncompressed state samples to \fIFILE\fP in the framed
zstd-compressed format consumed by
.BR goblin-skiff-compile-dictionary (1).
The file can contain terminal contents and should be treated as
sensitive.

.TP
.B \-\-state\-sample\-min\-size=\fIBYTES\fP
Only log received state samples at least this large. The default is 0.

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

.TP
.B MOSH_KEY
This variable must be set, and must contain a Base64-encoded cryptographic
key from
.BR goblin-skiff-server (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_ESCAPE_KEY
See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_PREDICTION_DISPLAY
Controls local echo as described in
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_PREDICTION_OVERWRITE
Controls local echo insert/overwrite as described in
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_TITLE_NOPREFIX
See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B DISPLAY
Local X display used when
.B \-X
is enabled.

.TP
.B XAUTHORITY
Local X authority file used for MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 rewriting when
.B \-X
is enabled. If unset, \fBgoblin-skiff-client\fP tries
.IR ~/.Xauthority .

.TP
.B MOSH_STREAM_DELAY
Default forwarded-stream coalescing delay in milliseconds. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_STREAM_BANDWIDTH
Default forwarded-stream payload-byte-per-second cap. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B MOSH_STATE_ZSTD_DICT
Default zstd state dictionary file. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B GOBLIN_SKIFF_STATE_SAMPLE_LOG
Default state sample log path. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

.TP
.B GOBLIN_SKIFF_STATE_SAMPLE_MIN_SIZE
Default minimum serialized state sample size to log. See
.BR goblin-skiff (1).

